            <ArticleTitle>A new method of continuous maxillary nerve block with an indwelling catheter.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>162-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We describe continuous maxillary nerve block at the pterygopalatine fossa and investigate the efficacy of this technique for perioperative pain control in oral surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixteen patients undergoing radical maxillary sinusotomy were enrolled; group I (n = 8) received general anesthesia without maxillary nerve block, and group II (n = 8) received maxillary nerve block before general anesthesia. The intraoperative concentration of sevoflurane, the extent of blockade, and total analgesics (diclofenac sodium) needed after surgery were recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean sevoflurane concentration was significantly lower in group II. Diclofenac sodium consumption was significantly reduced in group II.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Indwelling maxillary nerve catheter methods decrease anesthetic consumption and provide good postoperative analgesia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
